b:
Suffice to say I disagree on some of those.

That is fine, I fully empathize with the perspective of those who wanted to remain, I honestly nearly voted to remain and decided in the polling station. That said, I have hardened to my decision and do firmly believe it was the right one; I guess that is some facet of human nature.

Ultimately, my real belief is that 'What is democratic' and 'What is democracy' is subjective. If we accept that a democracy is defined by:

1. A political system for choosing and replacing the government through free and fair elections; 
2. The active participation of the people, as citizens, in politics and civic life; 
3. Protection of the human rights of all citizens;
4. A rule of law, in which the laws and procedures apply equally to all citizens.

Then I think the regional union scores highly on points 3 and 4. But I would also assert that it falls short heavily on point 2 and somewhat on point 1. Point 1 because I think the commission is the true government and the elected representatives are the scrutineers.

I am not saying the national system is perfect by those metrics, I just think we have a much better chance of changing it and the places it falls short are less immutable.
